Connect a Custom Domain to Vercel
Video 8 Script (same tone and structure as Video 1)

Today we are going to connect a real custom domain to this app.

No more random vercel.app URL.
A real domain.
With SSL.
Live.

This is a small step technically.
But a big step psychologically.

Here is what we are going to do.
We will buy a domain.
We will connect DNS.
We will verify SSL.
And we will redeploy.

That’s it.

The tools are simple.
Your domain provider.
Vercel.
And the project we already deployed.

I assume your app is working.
Authentication works.
Database works.
Stripe works.
Deployment is stable.

My name is Steve.
I was a professional software tester for over 12 years.
My job was to verify systems under real production conditions.
So today we make this look and behave like a real product.

Let’s start.

First step.
Buy a domain.

You can use any provider.
Namecheap.
Google Domains.
Whatever you prefer.

Pick a clean name.
Short if possible.
No weird spelling.

Once you own the domain, we go to Vercel.

Open your project.
Go to settings.
Go to domains.
Add your new domain.

Vercel will now give you DNS instructions.

Usually it’s one of two things.
An A record.
Or a CNAME record.

Now we go to your domain provider’s dashboard.

Open DNS settings.
Add the record exactly as Vercel shows it.
No guessing.
No improvising.

Save.

Now we wait a few minutes.

DNS propagation is not instant.
But usually it’s fast.

Back in Vercel, you will see the domain status.
Once it turns valid, Vercel automatically provisions SSL.

You don’t install certificates manually.
You don’t configure nginx.
It just works.

Now open your domain in the browser.

If everything is correct, your app loads.
With HTTPS.
With a lock icon.

No security warning.
No redirect loop.
Clean.

If it does not load, we check calmly.

Wrong DNS record.
Typo in domain.
Propagation delay.

We fix the root cause.
Not random settings.

Once it works, we redeploy once more.
Just to be clean.

Now open the site again.

This is no longer a test URL.
This is a product URL.

It feels different.

Not because the code changed.
But because perception changed.

This is practical credibility.

When you send this link to someone, it doesn’t look like a side project.
It looks intentional.

In the next video, we’ll tighten the production setup even more and remove the last rough edges before calling this a serious SaaS foundation.

See you there.